## Root cause
`Interval.fromISO` (src/interval.js) unconditionally called `DateTime.fromISO` on **both** halves of the string. With `Settings.throwOnInvalid = true`, `DateTime.fromISO` on the duration half (e.g. `"PT5H30M"`) throws `InvalidDateTimeError` instead of returning an invalid DateTime, so execution never reached the `Duration.fromISO` fallback branches — the valid duration/date interval was never constructed. Same for `Duration.fromISO` being unreachable in both orders.

## Fix (only file changed: /luxon/src/interval.js, static fromISO)
Probe each half with `DateTime.fromISO` inside try/catch, recording validity as a boolean; a throw is treated as "not a valid DateTime" so the duration branch is still reached. No public API/behavior change.

```diff
   static fromISO(text, opts) {
     const [s, e] = (text || "").split("/", 2);
     if (s && e) {
-      const start = DateTime.fromISO(s, opts),
+      let start, startIsValid;
+      try {
+        start = DateTime.fromISO(s, opts);
+        startIsValid = start.isValid;
+      } catch (e) {
+        startIsValid = false;
+      }
+
+      let end, endIsValid;
+      try {
         end = DateTime.fromISO(e, opts);
+        endIsValid = end.isValid;
+      } catch (e) {
+        endIsValid = false;
+      }

-      if (start.isValid && end.isValid) {
+      if (startIsValid && endIsValid) {
         return Interval.fromDateTimes(start, end);
       }

-      if (start.isValid) {
+      if (startIsValid) {
         const dur = Duration.fromISO(e, opts);
         if (dur.isValid) {
           return Interval.after(start, dur);
         }
-      } else if (end.isValid) {
+      } else if (endIsValid) {
         const dur = Duration.fromISO(s, opts);
         if (dur.isValid) {
           return Interval.before(end, dur);
```
